1 Data Cleaning is the process of identifying and correcting missing values, duplicate records, inconsistent formats, incorrect data types, outliers, and unwanted data to improve data quality before analysis or machine learning. Common steps include handling null values, removing duplicates, standardizing data, fixing data types, treating outliers, and saving the cleaned dataset. 

Data cleaning is important because raw data often contains errors, missing values, duplicates, inconsistencies, and incorrect formats. If we use such data directly, the analysis or machine learning model may produce incorrect results. 

Step 1: Import Required Libraries 

import pandas as pd                                     Used for data manipulation. 

import numpy as np                                      Used for numerical operations. 

Step 2: Load Dataset 

df = pd.read_csv("data.csv")                          Reads the CSV file into a DataFrame. 

Step 3: View Basic Information 

print(df.head())                                                   Shows first 5 rows. 

print(df.info())                                                     Displays column names, data types, and null values. 

print(df.describe())                                             statistical summary of numerical data. 

Step 4: Check Missing Values 

print(df.isnull().sum())                                      Counts missing values in each column. 

Step 5: Handle Missing Values 

A. Remove Missing Values 

df.dropna(inplace=True)                                 Deletes rows containing null values. 

B. Fill Missing Value 

df["Age"].fillna(df["Age"].mean(), inplace=True)         Replaces missing Age values with the average age. 

Step 6: Check Duplicate Records 

print(df.duplicated().sum())                                           Finds the number of duplicate rows. 

 

Step 7: Remove Duplicates 

df.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)                                   Removes repeated records. 

Step 8: Check Data Types 

print(df.dtypes                                                               Displays data type of each column. 

Step 9: Convert Data Type 

df["Age"] = df["Age"].astype(int)                            Converts Age column to integer type. 

Step 10: Rename Column Names 

df.rename(columns={"Emp_Name": "Employee_Name"}, inplace=True) 

 Changes column names for better readability. 

Step 11: Remove Extra Spaces 

df["Employee_Name"] = df["Employee_Name"].str.strip()   Removes leading and trailing spaces. 

Step 12: Standardize Text Values 

df["City"] = df["City"].str.upper()               Converts city names to uppercase for consistency. 

Step 13: Find Outliers 

Q1 = df["Salary"].quantile(0.25) 

Q3 = df["Salary"].quantile(0.75) 

IQR = Q3 - Q1 

  

outliers = df[(df["Salary"] < (Q1 - 1.5 * IQR)) | 

(df["Salary"] > (Q3 + 1.5 * IQR))] 

print(outliers) 
Uses the IQR method to identify unusual values. 

Step 14: Remove Outliers 

df = df[(df["Salary"] >= (Q1 - 1.5 * IQR)) &  (df["Salary"] <= (Q3 + 1.5 * IQR))]  Keeps only valid salary values. 

Step 15: Handle Inconsistent Data 

df["Gender"] = df["Gender"].replace({"M": "Male","F": "Female"})                            Standardizes gender values. 

Step 16: Check Unique Values 

print(df["Department"].unique())           Displays all unique department names. 

Step 17: Change Date Format 

df["Joining_Date"] = pd.to_datetime(df["Joining_Date"])       Converts string dates into datetime format. 

Step 18: Remove Unnecessary Columns 

df.drop(columns=["Temp_Column"], inplace=True)        Removes irrelevant columns. 

Step 19: Reset Index 

df.reset_index(drop=True, inplace=True)        Creates a new sequential index after cleaning. 

 

Step 20: Save Cleaned Dataset 

df.to_csv("cleaned_data.csv", index=False)               Stores the cleaned data into a new CSV file. 

 

 

